<PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">39125301</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2024</Year><Month>08</Month><Day>10</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2024</Year><Month>08</Month><Day>12</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">2072-6643</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>16</Volume><Issue>15</Issue><PubDate><Year>2024</Year><Month>Jul</Month><Day>25</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Nutrients</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Nutrients</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>The Importance of Vitamin K and the Combination of Vitamins K and D for Calcium Metabolism and Bone Health: A Review.</ArticleTitle><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">2420</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3390/nu16152420</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>The aim of the present review is to discuss the roles of vitamin K (phylloquinone or menaquinones) and vitamin K-dependent proteins, and the combined action of the vitamins K and D, for the maintenance of bone health. The most relevant vitamin K-dependent proteins in this respect are osteocalcin and matrix Gla-protein (MGP). When carboxylated, these proteins appear to have the ability to chelate and import calcium from the blood to the bone, thereby reducing the risk of osteoporosis. Carboxylated osteocalcin appears to contribute directly to bone quality and strength. An adequate vitamin K status is required for the carboxylation of MGP and osteocalcin. In addition, vitamin K acts on bone metabolism by other mechanisms, such as menaquinone 4 acting as a ligand for the nuclear steroid and xenobiotic receptor (SXR). In this narrative review, we examine the evidence for increased bone mineralization through the dietary adequacy of vitamin K. Summarizing the evidence for a synergistic effect of vitamin K and vitamin D3, we find that an adequate supply of vitamin K, on top of an optimal vitamin D status, seems to add to the benefit of maintaining bone health. More research related to synergism and the possible mechanisms of vitamins D3 and K interaction in bone health is needed.</AbstractText></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Aaseth</LastName><ForeName>Jan O</ForeName><Initials>JO</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-7518-5703</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Research, Innlandet Hospital Trust, P.O. Box 104, N-2381 Brumunddal, Norway.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Faculty of Health and Social Sciences, Inland Norway University of Applied Sciences, N-2418 Elverum, Norway.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Finnes</LastName><ForeName>Trine Elisabeth</ForeName><Initials>TE</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0003-1102-8706</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Medicine, Innlandet Hospital Hamar, P.O. Box 4453, N-2326 Hamar, Norway.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Endocrinology, Oslo University Hospital, P.O. Box 4950 Nydalen, N-0424 Oslo, Norway.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Askim</LastName><ForeName>Merete</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Independent Researcher, Bromstadvegen 43, N-7045 Trondheim, Norway.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Alexander</LastName><ForeName>Jan</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-6381-5720</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Norwegian Institute of Public Health, P.O. Box 222 Sk&#xf8;yen, N-0213 Oslo, Norway.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><GrantList CompleteYN="Y"><Grant><GrantID>No number</GrantID><Agency>Innlandet Hospital Trust</Agency><Country/></Grant><Grant><GrantID>No Number</GrantID><Agency>Innlandet Hospital Trust</Agency><Country/></Grant></G